WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:10.230
- Welcome, everyone,

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.310
we have the pleasure today

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:17.860
of having Professor Elena Fiddian-Qasmiyeh

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.593
from University College London,

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:26.880
she is the professor in
Migration and Refugee Studies

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.230
at the Geography Department

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.640
in, at UCL, in the UK.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.640
She's the co-director of the
UCL Migration Research Unit

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.220
and is the founder and director

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.990
of the Institute of Advanced Studies,

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:49.990
Refugee in a Moving World
research network across UCL.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.200
So Professor Qasmiyeh she
has a vast experience,

00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.373
focusing

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:05.120
on the study of the refugees

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.220
and the intersection between
gender generation and religion

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.460
in context of conflict
induced displacement

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:17.743
with a particular focus on the
Middle East and North Africa.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.060
She has conducted extensive
research in refugee camps

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:27.650
and urban areas included the
Nigeria, Cuba, Egypt, France,

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:32.453
Jordan, Lebanon, South Africa,
Syria, Sweden and the UK.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.720
Drawing on a critical
theoretical perspective,

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.750
her work contributes to key debates

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.760
surrounding refugees experiences

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.250
of conflict induced displacement,

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.880
the nature of refugee (murmurs) relation

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.080
and both North-South and
South-South Humanitarian Responses

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.870
to forced migration.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:57.440
Her current research
examines Southern responses

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.290
to displacement from Syria,

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:05.750
including responses developed
by local host communities

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.010
and refugee themselves.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:12.840
So let's welcome
Professor Fiddian-Qasmiyeh

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.930
and she will be first
giving her presentation

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:21.093
and then she will be taking
questions at the end.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.560
- Wonderful, thank you so much

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.360
for your very warm words of welcome

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.690
and thank you for inviting
me, thanks to all of you

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.530
for joining the session.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.020
I know that there are many
competing demands on your time

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:37.830
so I'm very glad to see
so many names in squares

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.780
on my screen.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.340
So today I'm going to
be speaking on the topic

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.442
of "Refugee-led Responses
to Overlapping Precarities",

00:02:47.442 --> 00:02:51.340
focusing on a particular
refugee camp in North Lebanon

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:56.340
and in so doing, I'll be
drawing on two articles,

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:01.420
which are themselves based
on longstanding research.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:04.090
So these articles were
published last year,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.210
firstly in the Journal
of Palestine Studies,

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:10.430
which focuses in particular
on the evolving situation

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:15.430
with reference to coronavirus,
COVID-19 from March to June

00:03:17.043 --> 00:03:19.207
in Baddawi Refugee Camp in North Lebanon

00:03:19.207 --> 00:03:21.330
and the second piece,
which has co-authored

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.350
with Yousif Mustafah Qasmiyeh,

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.270
who was himself born
in Baddawi Refugee Camp

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.340
as an article that was
published in Current History

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.970
and focuses in part

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:35.130
on the events surrounding
the post-Beirut blast

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:40.130
in August, 2020 and again,
how this has affected people

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:43.520
who've experienced
displacement and are responding

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:47.693
to overlapping crises effectively
in their neighborhoods.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.780
These articles are themselves informed

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.470
by longstanding research
as I've mentioned,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:59.300
including two research
projects, "Refugee Hosts"

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.880
and "Southern Responses to Displacement",

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:04.560
which I've been leading since 2016

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.660
with a team of colleagues,
researchers in Lebanon, Jordan

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.190
and Turkey, as well as in Europe

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.350
and which have been
exploring how people, groups,

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:17.530
organizations in the Middle
East amongst other places

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:21.573
have been responding to
displacement from Syria since 2011.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.330
And as I hope is increasingly
recognized by now,

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.900
far from being passive
recipients of humanitarian aid,

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.170
people who have experienced displacement

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.490
are themselves always already
finding ways to respond

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.760
to the challenges that they face

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:42.470
and also to help others
in the same situations.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.660
And in those projects, "Refugee Hosts"

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.940
and "Southern Responses to Displacement",

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.340
we start from the premise

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.370
that the residents of neighborhoods,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.910
which are hosting refugees,

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:53.930
have often experienced displacement

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:56.500
and forced migration
in the past themselves

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.660
and indeed may well face
displacement again in the future.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.250
So this effectively means

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:06.310
that people who are currently
labeled as refugees, were,

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.490
are and will be hosts

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.530
and the ways that histories
of displacement intersect

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.930
with histories of hosting,
have important implications

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.710
for both the present and the future.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.820
And in my research, this
means increasing attention

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.210
to different forms of
refugee-refugee relationality,

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:29.270
which is a term that I use to
explore these relationships

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:32.660
between people who have
been, been displaced

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:37.530
and that includes a focus on
refugees, hosting refugees

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.890
and on a broader range
of refugee-led responses

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.670
to displacement and indeed their responses

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:45.363
to more than displacement.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.955
And there's focus on the
more than displacement,

00:05:48.955 --> 00:05:52.575
I think is important
for a number of reasons.

00:05:52.575 --> 00:05:56.350
And this focus on the
refugee-refugee relationality

00:05:56.350 --> 00:06:00.230
in the context of displacement
and more than displacement,

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.550
is important because it's solely focusing

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:06.490
on and providing aid to
newly displaced people

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:09.070
or people who have just
experienced a crisis

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:11.830
in the short term as it
were, can discriminate

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:14.990
against those who are living
in protracted situations

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:19.220
of displacement, whose vulnerability
may increase over time.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.870
And this is one of the key challenges

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:24.120
that I think that we face
when policy and practice

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.220
is often characterized

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.190
by what we can call a present test bias

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:30.530
that is only focusing on and responding

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:32.740
to the new crisis situation

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.080
rather than thinking more
carefully about intersecting

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.483
and overlapping forms of precarity.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:43.050
So when we think about these
overlapping precarities

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.960
and the increasing vulnerability
over time, unfortunately

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.730
the situation in Lebanon
demonstrates this very clearly.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:53.810
So we can start by noting of course,

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:57.750
that displacement from Syria to Lebanon

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:01.680
may have started in 2011
but of course takes place

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.930
on top of longstanding
protracted displacements

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.423
both internally and internationally.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:12.150
When we then look at the events of 2020,

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:15.560
we see that these starkly
illustrate in the case of Lebanon,

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.650
the ways that vulnerabilities
can be accentuated

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:23.000
by intersecting crises, such
as the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.320
which has afflicted
the country since March

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.330
and which has led to a range of xenophobic

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:30.910
and discriminatory responses,

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:34.640
including discriminate,
discriminatory curfews on refugees,

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.470
politicians demanding camp
closures and falsely asserting

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:42.240
that refugees were vectors of
disease under the contagion

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.420
when in fact we know that
COVID-19 arrived in Lebanon

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.890
through Lebanese citizens
travel from Italy and around

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:51.530
but using this well-worn trope

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:53.710
of refugees as vectors of disease

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:57.480
to justify increased
surveillance and control

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.823
through these xenophobic
and discriminatory policies.

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:04.760
So on top of that, we then
have the devastating explosion

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:08.170
in Beirut's port on the
4th of August last year,

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:11.220
which was believed to have been
caused by an impounded cargo

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.390
of ammonium nitrate stored for
several years in a warehouse

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:17.030
without proper safety precautions.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:20.610
And with major loss of life
and significant injuries,

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.870
alongside the destruction
to the port and the roads,

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:26.730
this has made the emergency
delivery of aid supplies

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:30.340
both very important but
also increasingly difficult

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:33.250
and international donors
started to send aid,

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:36.540
including medical supplies
and equipment to Lebanon

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:39.270
via the smaller Northern port of Tripoli,

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.563
which we'll be turning to shortly.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:46.020
At that point, this is August
last year, at this point

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.930
the Lebanese Red Cross
headquarters announces

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.130
that all of the groups, hospital teams

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.270
in the North of the country
would be relocated to Beirut

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.460
due to a shift in priorities

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:59.080
and they were needed to
bolster medical capacity

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.250
to assess both blast survivors

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.500
and the dramatically increasing number

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:06.193
of COVID-19 patients in the Capitol.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:09.890
So bearing that background in mind,

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:11.830
it's now to the coronavirus pandemic

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:14.050
that will turn, shifting from Beirut,

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:16.410
which has been the focus
of so much attention,

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:18.790
to a small and relatively
under researched,

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:21.683
urban refugee camp in North
Lebanon, called Baddawi Camp.

00:09:23.233 --> 00:09:27.680
Baddawi Refugee Camp was
established in the early 1950s

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.360
and it is here that residents
have been supporting

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.720
both, in inverted commas,
old and new fellow refugees

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.910
who share the camp amidst
the multifaceted risks

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:42.280
created by the pandemic and by
the diverse responses to it,

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:45.010
responses that include unequal allocation

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.700
of and access to healthcare resources

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.710
in addition to anti-refugee
political reference,

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:54.460
rhetoric and policies.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:55.360
So there are many ways

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.780
that we could introduce
Baddawi Refugee Camp

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:01.797
but here I'll draw on
Yousif M. Qasmiyeh's poem,

00:10:01.797 --> 00:10:03.060
"Writing the Camp",

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:06.123
which I think captures
some of its key dynamics.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.940
So he writes, "Refugees ask
other refugees, 'Who are you,

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.470
who are we to come to you and
who are you to come to us?'

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:20.060
Nobody answers, Palestinians,
Syrians, Iraqis, Kurds

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:23.960
share the camp, the same different
camp, the camp of a camp.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.470
They've all come to
re-originate the beginning

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.487
with their own hands and feet".

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:32.460
And this reference to
re-originating the beginning

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:37.190
with their own hands and feet
is a very important reminder

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:38.900
that for many people

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.240
who've experienced
conflict and displacement,

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:44.030
they are always already in
the middle of displacement,

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:45.910
they have to re-originate the beginning

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:48.490
because it is a recurring process.

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:50.970
In the case of Baddawi Refugee Camp,

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:53.740
about 15,000 Palestinian refugees

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.360
who had previously been living

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.670
in the Nahr el-Bared
Refugee Camp in Lebanon,

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.220
were internally displaced too

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:02.550
and subsequently hosted in
Baddawi following the destruction

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:05.083
of Nahr el-Bared Camp in 2007.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:10.180
This means that around 10,000
refugees from Nahr el-Bared,

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:13.430
are now part of the refugee
community in Baddawi Camp

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:16.680
who are hosting people who've
been displaced from Syria

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.930
and who are responding to
the needs and the rights

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:20.980
of the diverse residents of Baddawi Camp.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:25.070
So here we have internally
displaced refugees,

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:29.450
hosted by refugees who are
themselves hosting refugees

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:31.850
and this is a very
important again, reminder

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.850
of the precarity of the
situations that people live in

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:38.260
but also these intersecting
processes of displacement

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:43.260
and of hosting and of the
multiple crises effectively

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:46.680
that people navigate
in their everyday lives

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.200
and in the lives of their families

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:50.363
and those who live around them.

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:54.010
Now, it was early in 2010

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:57.490
as the Coronavirus started to erupt

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:01.180
in different, different spaces
and in different guises,

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:03.510
that the residents of
Baddawi Camp themselves,

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:08.240
started to await with fear
for the arrival of COVID-19,

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:11.870
as Qasmiyeh writes in his
poem, "With a Third Eye,

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:15.257
I See the Catastrophe", so
I'll read his lines here,

00:12:15.257 --> 00:12:18.360
"As we wait for the
disease, in echoless rooms,

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:22.720
doors locked up, shutters
dusted, thrust to the heart.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:26.210
The disease that will sign
a pact with our diseases.

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:28.960
In patience bereft of patience,

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:32.460
we stand still behind our
walls: without seeing,

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:35.150
we shall see the disease that will be.

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:37.967
With a third eye, I see the catastrophe".

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:43.160
And this fear has been born
out by the UN statistics,

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:45.370
which in February of this year,

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:49.500
documented the Palestinians
COVID-19 mortality rate

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.840
is three times higher
than the mortality rate

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:53.403
for the country as a whole.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:58.070
But importantly as we all
know, this increased risk,

00:12:58.070 --> 00:12:59.740
this increased mortality risk,

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:03.330
is not because of displacement,
it isn't inevitable.

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:06.820
Instead, camp residents in
Lebanon are acutely vulnerable

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:08.930
to the impacts of COVID-19

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:12.050
because of, living in
overcrowded urban camps

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:16.100
with poor infrastructure,
many residents afflicted

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:18.990
with pre-existing health
conditions, have multiplied

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:22.420
and worsened over time due
to, amongst other things,

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:25.650
the fragile UNRWA health
systems, which have been defunded

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:28.540
and weakened over
decades by fickle donors,

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:31.060
while Lebanese health
systems have a long history

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:36.000
of discriminating against and
denying treatment to refugees.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.030
So against this backdrop, it's
not surprising that mistrust

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:41.800
amongst Palestinians and
their fellow camp dwellers

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:44.520
towards international
and national aid agencies

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:47.040
and government departments,
has remained very high

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:51.170
as they have faced COVID-19,
which leads us to the question,

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:52.263
well, how have the residents

00:13:52.263 --> 00:13:54.530
of Baddawi Refugee Camp responded

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.493
to this imminent catastrophe?

00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:00.880
Well, indeed refugees in
Lebanon, like other displaced

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:02.610
and dispossessed people around the world,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:05.440
have drawn on longstanding
traditions of mutual aid

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:08.360
and of solidarity to
seek and to enact ways

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:11.560
to protect themselves and
others from the pandemic

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.633
and I'll turn now briefly
to some of those responses.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.760
So the first example that I'll dwell on

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:22.360
is from March, 2020.

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:24.980
So this is more or less coinciding

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.350
with politicians declarations

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:29.650
that camps are dangerous spaces

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:31.990
and that camps should be closed off

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:34.560
and that curfew should be
imposed by the government

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:38.910
and enacted by Lebanese security forces,

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:42.230
which did in fact take
place across the country

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:45.074
but on the 22nd of March last year,

00:14:45.074 --> 00:14:48.420
Baddawi Camp's Joint
Security Forces Committee,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.350
in addition to the Popular Committee

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:52.880
and the Mosques Committee,
jointly announced

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.390
that a curfew would be enforced

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:56.870
on the camp's residents and businesses

00:14:56.870 --> 00:15:00.400
and unlike previous
curfews and camp lockdowns,

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:03.220
of which there have been many
since the camps establishment

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:06.560
in the 1950s, this curfew wasn't imposed

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:09.100
by Lebanese authorities
asserting that the camps

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:11.010
are sites of danger,

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:14.490
instead, because COVID-19
cases had been confirmed

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.320
in a neighboring area,
Baddawi Camp activists

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.470
in fact, lobbied for the
camps entrances and exits

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:23.893
to be closed in order to
help protect camp residents.

00:15:24.893 --> 00:15:28.420
And Baddawi as in other
displacement and hosting contexts,

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:31.510
refugee camps are not isolated spaces

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:34.500
but instead are intimately
connected to urban

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.903
and non-urban areas beyond
the boundaries of the camp.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:41.890
So although Palestinian camps
have often been perceived

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:45.600
as islands of insecurity
to use Rose Misaya's term,

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:48.930
in this context, many camp
residents promptly identified

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:51.690
the risks that existed outside of the camp

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:54.993
and worked to encourage people
to remain within their homes.

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:59.930
So a second example of
camp-based responses,

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:01.743
relates to information.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:05.360
So UNRWA had been perceived
as being very slow

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:09.510
to inform camp residents
about the risks of COVID-19

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:11.730
and had also been perceived
as being very slow

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:14.530
to provide different forms of support.

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:18.420
So members of the Palestinian
Cultural Club in Baddawi Camp,

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:21.480
the Baddawi Camp Cultural
Club as I'll refer to it now,

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:22.930
they promptly took action,

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:25.810
drawing on that intimate
knowledge of camp life

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.610
and of everyday needs
and rights in the camp

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:33.300
to adapt to existing evidence-based
informational posters

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:36.070
including World Health
Organization materials,

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:39.200
translating them into Arabic
and sharing these posters

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:43.300
and other forms of guidance
in print and via social media

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:45.350
to reach camp residents
of all demographics

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:47.430
in an accessible manner.

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:49.210
In addition to running special programs

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:52.320
on it's radio station,
the cultural club worked

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.650
with social media networks

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:56.250
that have been established
locally for years,

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:59.470
these networks previously had
been used to inform residents

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:02.270
about everything from school
closures, to which areas

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:05.090
of the camp should be
avoided during armed clashes

00:17:05.090 --> 00:17:07.080
or sporadic shootings.

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:10.610
Now, coronavirus at this
stage had not entered the camp

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:12.980
but there was a very strong recognition

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:15.430
that although it wasn't visible or audible

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:18.610
in the way that these armed
clashes and sporadic shootings

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:19.960
had been in the past,

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:23.580
that it was an equally
significant existential threat

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:25.450
to residents in the camp.

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:28.950
So this sharing of information
was, was very important

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:30.083
from that perspective.

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:35.610
By April, they also
increasingly focused on food,

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.180
which of course is an essential resource

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:41.070
and many camp residents
have for a very long time

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:44.340
in the camp, tried to make
sure that its diverse,

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:47.410
that the camp's diverse
residents receive enough food

00:17:48.399 --> 00:17:51.190
at different junctures of their lives.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:52.640
And over the past few years

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:55.130
in preparation for the
Holy month of Ramadan,

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:56.210
Palestinian residents,

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:58.410
including members of the cultural club,

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:01.710
have collected financial
donations from other Palestinians

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:05.290
to prepare Iftar food
baskets to break the fast

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:08.400
during the month of
Ramadan, to be distributed

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:11.550
to residents identified
as especially in need,

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:13.030
whatever their nationality.

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:16.470
So Palestinian, Syrian,
Iraqi, Kurdish or Lebanese,

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.923
and irrespective of their place of origin.

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:24.320
In 2020, given the fears and
the restrictions on movement

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:26.660
which arose due to the pandemic,

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:30.200
the organizers of these
food baskets were concerned

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:32.500
that it would not be safe
for large numbers of people

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:35.050
to go door to door, collecting donations

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:37.740
or to work together shopping
for ingredients, cooking

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.863
and subsequently distributing
hot meals during Ramadan.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:46.100
But nonetheless, the number
of people in need of food,

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.640
has been much larger
than in previous years,

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:50.500
not just because of the pandemic

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:52.650
but also due to the ongoing collapse

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:56.350
of the already fragile Lebanese economy.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:18:59.100
So the camp was able to receive donations,

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:03.610
sourced from the camp itself
and from the neighboring areas,

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:08.610
started by distributing food
boxes because of this concern

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:11.450
that it wouldn't be safe to congregate

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:14.360
and to prepare food and
then distribute it together

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:17.790
but then realized increasingly,
that for many people

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:19.420
who would particularly benefit

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:21.770
from receiving these food baskets,

00:19:21.770 --> 00:19:24.720
that they may not in
fact have the resources

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:27.120
to be able to cook the
food, they might not have

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:32.080
the cooking equipment, they
might not have fridges,

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:35.740
access to electricity or
cooking stoves, et cetera,

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:37.080
to be able to cook this.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:41.760
So the decision was made
to enact food preparation

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:45.420
in a safe manner and to
subsequently distribute food

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:48.450
through respecting social
distancing measures

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.100
as far as possible.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:53.060
So they adapted to this changing situation

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:58.060
and expanded preexisting
"Ramadan Basket" initiative

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:01.090
to ensure that the largest
number of people in the camp

00:20:01.090 --> 00:20:03.953
were able to access
these, these food baskets.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:08.200
But just a few days before
the start of Ramadan

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:11.900
on April 23rd, as the
limits of local resources

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:13.680
became increasingly apparent,

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:17.160
the Baddawi Camp Cultural Club chapters,

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:18.070
well, the chapter in Baddawi

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:21.530
but also the cultural club
chapter in Mar Elias Camp,

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:24.240
joined an emergency relief fundraiser,

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:28.220
in partnership with a center
in Burj al-Barajneh Camp

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:31.510
and also two US-based
Palestinian initiatives,

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:33.330
the Palestinian Youth Movement

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:36.810
and The Palestine Rights
to Return Coalition

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:40.620
and their aim was to raise $50,000, to buy

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:45.060
and distribute packages of
food and sanitizing products

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:48.620
in order to help residents
of those three camps;

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.270
Baddawi, Mar Elias and Burj al-Barajneh

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:53.280
to be able to practice social distancing

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:55.640
and offset some of the economic damage,

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:58.110
which had been caused by the pandemic

00:20:58.110 --> 00:20:59.590
and within three months,

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:02.420
this initiative had exceeded that goal.

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:06.810
That international collaboration
was also supplemented

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:09.310
with the cultural clubs initiative,

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:12.310
which was a "Who Will Donate
a Million?" fundraising drive

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:16.770
which was broad, broadcast on
their social media channels

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:18.340
and on the internet.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:21.650
Now, of course as I'm talking
about this, it's an important,

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:24.720
it's very important to note
that need and vulnerability

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:27.370
do not simply arise
because of displacement

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:30.530
but rather to remind ourselves
that precarities produced

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:32.090
and is not inevitable.

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:35.020
So vulnerability to shocks and crises

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:39.250
will increase over time and
increase due to different crises

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:43.480
including the spiraling crisis

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:45.910
of Lebanon's financial system.

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:48.950
So here we have some of
the key dates which exist

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:53.950
or coincide with the arrival
of, of COVID 19 in, in Lebanon

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:57.710
with bank transfers and bank
withdrawals being suspended

00:21:57.710 --> 00:22:01.370
in March, 2020, the
closure of Beirut Airport

00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:04.090
and the devaluation of the Lebanese Lira,

00:22:04.090 --> 00:22:08.880
all meaning that people were
unable to access, access money,

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:11.410
if they had access to,
previously had access

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:15.340
to bank accounts, they were
unable to receive remittances

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:19.020
from relatives and
friends outside of Lebanon

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.930
and whatever money they
did have access to,

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:25.480
was increasingly unable
to purchase the goods,

00:22:25.480 --> 00:22:27.610
which themselves were
also limited in supply

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:29.020
for different reasons.

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:32.330
So here we see that the risk
of people becoming destitute

00:22:32.330 --> 00:22:36.020
and starving is because of
policies and political decisions,

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:38.320
not just because of
displacement as it were.

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.740
And ultimately, social
distancing is impossible

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:45.440
for people who live in
overcrowded conditions,

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:47.820
who have no savings to draw on to buy food

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.940
and whose survival is contingent
on precarious daily labor

00:22:51.940 --> 00:22:53.730
in the informal sector.

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:56.050
And indeed in Lebanon,
national authorities

00:22:56.050 --> 00:22:59.270
have long prohibited
Palestinian and Syrian refugees

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:02.720
from entering the formal labor
market and that leaves them

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:06.330
amongst the people least
likely to be able to afford

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:09.240
to practice social distancing,
therefore putting them

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:11.753
at higher risk of contracting the disease.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:16.760
So with this brief chronology
that I'm going through,

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:19.710
in fact, by June, 2020,

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:23.310
there were these diverse
camp-based activities

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:26.010
and camp-led activities,
which had been taking place

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:28.080
in Baddawi Camp but at this point,

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:30.690
no Palestinians in Lebanon
had yet been confirmed

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.233
as contracting or having contracted COVID.

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:37.370
But unfortunately on
21st of June last year,

00:23:37.370 --> 00:23:39.970
state-run National News Agency reported

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:42.540
that municipal testing of Syrian refugees

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:45.720
working in Tripoli's port, had indicated

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:48.300
that a number of Syrians
who lived in Baddawi Camp

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:51.600
and on the camp's outskirts
had tested positive.

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:55.600
And at this stage, UNRWA representatives

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:59.300
in conjunction with
these media declarations,

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:03.070
indicated that the camps confirmed cases

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:06.520
marked a significant tipping
point for the whole country

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:09.277
and one UNRWA representative stated,

00:24:09.277 --> 00:24:12.720
"The epidemic no longer
just threatens our camps,

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:14.417
it has now entered them".

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:18.990
So at this stage, we witnessed
discriminatory processes

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:22.690
including nationality-based
testing systems

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:23.970
and these processes led

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:27.040
to UNRWA officials implicitly asserting

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:31.100
that the previously clean
Barajneh camps, (mumbles) Camp,

00:24:31.100 --> 00:24:34.740
was now at risk due to
the Syrians infections.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:38.240
So Syrian refugees were
identified by the authorities

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:41.110
and by the media as the vector of disease

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:43.620
and in turn, local actors in the camp

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:48.120
including Palestinian factions
and the Popular Committee,

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:50.920
announced that they had
identified the location

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:54.720
of the two Syrians living
quarters within Baddawi Camp

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:57.780
and marked them as the
epicenter of contagion risk

00:24:57.780 --> 00:24:58.793
within the camp.

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:03.450
At this time, contact tracing
was immediately set in motion

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:05.700
under the supervision of
the camp's health committee

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:07.640
and the Lebanese Ministry of Health,

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:10.220
which had committed to
transferring any individuals

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:13.320
who tested positive to a state hospital.

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:15.980
But as these developments unfolded,

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:19.230
some camp residents started
to associate COVID-19

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:22.720
with Syrians in line with
the xenophobic rhetoric

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:25.040
that had been used by Lebanese politicians

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:26.903
at the onset of the pandemic.

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.910
And amid the raising, the rising fear

00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:32.650
of the arrival of the virus,

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:35.570
members of the popular and
the security committees,

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:39.230
started roaming the alleyways
and the streets of the camp,

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:41.320
instructing people to shutter their shops

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:42.890
and to stay indoors

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:47.190
and Baddawi Camp was once
again subjected to closure,

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:49.713
a space under community in quarantine.

00:25:50.770 --> 00:25:54.550
Again, this is a good point to,
to step back and to remember

00:25:54.550 --> 00:25:58.620
that local responses
developed in Baddawi Camp,

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:01.600
including those funded
locally and those supported

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:04.480
through transnational
networks, demonstrate the ways

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:06.730
that camp residents
have worked individually

00:26:06.730 --> 00:26:10.360
and collectively to find
ways to care for Palestinian,

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:14.340
Syrian, Iraqi, Kurdish and
Lebanese residents alike,

00:26:14.340 --> 00:26:16.050
therefore transcending a focus

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:19.600
on nationality-based identity markers.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:23.150
And yet, such modes of
solidarity and mutuality

00:26:23.150 --> 00:26:26.090
have been placed at risk
precisely due to national

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:29.310
and municipal interventions
that align with policies

00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:31.080
and with political discourses

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:33.100
that have constituted the refugee "Other"

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:36.520
as the threatening carrier
of disease and risk.

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:39.870
So in essence, tensions
and hostility are produced,

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:41.063
not inevitable.

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:45.200
So the final example that I'll give now

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:48.690
of refugee-led responses
arises in the context

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:52.630
of the aftermath of the
devastating August, 2020 blast

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:55.490
in Beirut's port, which
I referred to earlier

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:56.940
and this coincided

00:26:56.940 --> 00:27:00.980
with the Coronavirus infection
rates soaring across Lebanon

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:03.260
and with medical infrastructure nationwide

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:04.823
being under mounting pressure.

00:27:05.710 --> 00:27:08.870
So in autumn 2020, all of the Red Cross

00:27:08.870 --> 00:27:10.910
and Red Crescent
hospitals in North Lebanon

00:27:10.910 --> 00:27:14.620
started to relocate with medical
staff either being laid off

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:15.920
or moving to Beirut

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:18.630
in order to care for both blasts survivors

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:21.610
and for the rising number
of COVID-19 patients

00:27:21.610 --> 00:27:23.120
in the Capitol.

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:25.320
And although, although
that focus on Beirut

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:28.060
is very understandable,
given the vast destruction

00:27:28.060 --> 00:27:30.250
caused by the port explosion,

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:32.610
allocating more resources to the Capitol

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:35.520
has put people living
elsewhere in the country

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:38.623
at greater risk, citizens
and refugees alike.

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:42.040
So recognizing the increasing pressure

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:43.810
on medical infrastructure

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:46.910
and the national, amidst
this national emergency

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:50.400
and as infection rates and
deaths started to increase

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:53.980
in Baddawi Refugee Camp, the
Baddawi Camp Cultural Club

00:27:53.980 --> 00:27:57.930
approached local officials
in the town of Baddawi

00:27:57.930 --> 00:27:59.770
in August to ask if they could use

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:02.980
one of the municipalities ambulances

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:07.690
in order to take refugees with
COVID symptoms to hospitals

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:10.270
and the municipality grant its approval,

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:13.070
loaning the company ambulance
that was not being used

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:14.690
at that time.

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:17.760
So a member of the Baddawi
Camp Cultural Club volunteered

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:21.990
to collect people who have
suspected COVID-19 infections

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:24.750
from both Baddawi Camp and
also the neighboring camp,

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:28.770
Nahr el-Bared and volunteered
to transport them to hospitals

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:31.130
in Tripoli and in Nahr
el-Bared in some cases,

00:28:31.130 --> 00:28:33.630
to the Rafik Hariri Hospital in Beirut,

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:37.520
where the majority of COVID
cases have been cared for.

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:40.520
So this Palestinian refugee driver,

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:44.980
if we want to use that
term, wears a hazmat suit

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:46.120
and protective gear

00:28:46.120 --> 00:28:48.220
which have been provided by the Red Cross

00:28:48.220 --> 00:28:51.350
and by the Red Crescent
Society at the club's request,

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:53.980
masks have been bought
from local supermarkets

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:58.180
and from shops, both in the
camp and from neighboring areas

00:28:58.180 --> 00:29:01.480
and by borrowing this
ambulance, the cultural club

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:04.660
has been using what we would
call or what Yousif and I call

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:06.800
the, "using the citizens tools

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:09.337
in order to assist other refugees".

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:13.790
Now in doing so, the,
the camp's cultural club

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:16.610
and its members, have
continued to improvise

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:19.050
with the limited available resources

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:22.713
to navigate entrenched
inequitable systems.

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:26.900
Such makeshift measures
however, are not sustainable

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:29.730
and there are no substitute
for the health care resources

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:33.350
that national and local
governments should be allocating

00:29:33.350 --> 00:29:34.970
to the camps during this crisis

00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:36.720
to protect both their residents

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:39.320
and the surrounding communities.

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:43.800
So our interlocutors
have consistently noted

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:46.170
that they celebrate the extent

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:48.820
to which the local responses
have been developed

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:52.780
but they also reassert that
these are not sustainable

00:29:52.780 --> 00:29:55.320
and that in fact, more
pressure needs to be applied

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:58.250
to ensure that the structural inequalities

00:29:58.250 --> 00:29:59.590
that prevent people

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:01.660
from being able to enact
their own decisions

00:30:01.660 --> 00:30:03.680
and to be able to protect their own lives,

00:30:03.680 --> 00:30:06.100
those structural barriers
need to be challenged

00:30:06.100 --> 00:30:09.060
and that more equitable
systems need to be developed

00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:12.780
to provide people with
access to, meaningful access

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:15.300
to medical supplies, medical care,

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:19.320
in addition to fair access to
the labor market, for example.

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:22.220
So these are structural issues
that need to be focused on.

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:27.350
So in conclusion, documenting or archiving

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:32.350
following use of Qasmiyeh's
term, these local responses

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:34.810
is a vital means of stressing the ways

00:30:34.810 --> 00:30:36.730
that people who have been displaced,

00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:40.310
themselves seek to fill the
gaps and to redress inequalities

00:30:40.310 --> 00:30:42.630
that have been created and reproduced

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:45.880
by national and
international actors alike.

00:30:45.880 --> 00:30:49.050
And well before COVID-19
arrived in Lebanon,

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:51.017
mutual aid initiatives in Baddawi Camp

00:30:51.017 --> 00:30:52.710
and of course elsewhere,

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:55.610
had transcended
nationality-based identifiers

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:57.820
to provide support to camp residents

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.780
with wide-ranging backgrounds

00:30:59.780 --> 00:31:02.870
in turn highlighting the
importance of critically examining

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:06.390
the ways that preexisting
support systems and networks

00:31:06.390 --> 00:31:09.200
are being affected both
by the virus itself

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:12.250
and by the different policies
and politics emerging

00:31:12.250 --> 00:31:14.040
in the context of the pandemic

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:17.980
including nationality-based
testing and overlapping crises

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:19.693
such as the Beirut blast.

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:23.840
So ultimately, discourses and policies

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:26.350
that demarcate the sanctity of the "Self"

00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:28.490
through the denigration of the "Other",

00:31:28.490 --> 00:31:31.710
heighten the dangers of
politically-produced precarity

00:31:31.710 --> 00:31:34.230
while undermining the
potential for solidarity

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:36.410
in times of overlapping precarities

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:41.370
However, individuals, communities
and civil society groups

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:45.000
continue finding and implementing
those makeshift measures

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:47.450
which aim to fill the gaps
which have been created

00:31:47.450 --> 00:31:50.620
by national and
international actors alike.

00:31:50.620 --> 00:31:52.710
And in essence, we cannot understand

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:56.310
either the vulnerabilities that
people face in displacement

00:31:56.310 --> 00:31:59.120
or the responses that they are developing

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:02.120
without considering the
ways that local experiences

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:06.230
of and responses to
displacement and other crises,

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:09.610
are framed by national
and international systems

00:32:09.610 --> 00:32:12.460
including longstanding
structural inequalities

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:16.170
and processes of
marginalization and exclusion.

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:19.370
So the vulnerabilities that
the residents of Baddawi Camp,

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:22.480
Palestinians and Syrians
alongside Iraqis, Kurds

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:26.210
and Lebanese are facing, are
caused by political failures

00:32:26.210 --> 00:32:28.380
that have deep historical roots

00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:32.000
and whose repercussions will
continue to be palpably felt

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:34.273
in both the near and the distant future.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:37.060
So thank you again so much for listening

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:39.960
and I'm very much looking
forward to our conversations

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:44.056
and our discussions, I hope
we've got enough time now

00:32:44.056 --> 00:32:48.230
for some exchange and I'd
also like to encourage you

00:32:48.230 --> 00:32:50.780
to please join our
communities of conversation,

00:32:50.780 --> 00:32:53.750
both on the Refugee Hosts platform

00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:57.070
and on the Southern Responses
to Displacement platform,

00:32:57.070 --> 00:33:01.200
both of whose contact details
you can see on the slide here

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:03.550
which we'll just leave up for a moment

00:33:03.550 --> 00:33:06.200
and then I will stop sharing the screen

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:09.110
and come back to see all of you now.

00:33:09.110 --> 00:33:11.800
So thank you again so
very much for listening.

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:12.633
Thank you.

00:33:12.633 --> 00:33:14.930
- Yes, so thank you very much,

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:18.150
so I don't know if you have questions,

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:23.150
you're welcome to either
