Pacific kind of examples

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:43.270
and how that does or doesn't challenge

00:39:43.270 --> 00:39:44.930
those systems themselves

00:39:44.930 --> 00:39:47.564
and your work is really
important in that Louisa,

00:39:47.564 --> 00:39:48.397
thank you.

00:39:49.600 --> 00:39:52.620
- Just, I'm just struck by
the tension of the refugees

00:39:52.620 --> 00:39:55.030
who are showing that they are capable,

00:39:55.030 --> 00:39:58.540
that they are able to offer
the solutions to problems

00:39:58.540 --> 00:40:01.490
that they're not simply a social burden

00:40:01.490 --> 00:40:05.300
and yet not wanting to
aggrandize themselves

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:07.563
to the point where it's
sort of like and so,

00:40:08.530 --> 00:40:11.320
there isn't still this
existing, incredible inequality.

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:13.830
In other words, that, that's
attention that I found

00:40:13.830 --> 00:40:15.610
in my own field work in Greece as well,

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:19.100
where refugees are saying,
"Yes, we are, we can do it

00:40:19.100 --> 00:40:22.250
but also this is an impossible task".

00:40:22.250 --> 00:40:24.850
And I just, you started to
sort of talk a little bit

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:25.683
about that towards the end

00:40:25.683 --> 00:40:28.110
and I just wanted to
get more, I don't know,

00:40:28.110 --> 00:40:30.470
ethnographic grounding in what
your, some of the specifics

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:32.930
if you could give us some,
so that I love the image

00:40:32.930 --> 00:40:37.360
that you had of the cords
all wrapped around this stick

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:39.400
and you just, people are so creative

00:40:39.400 --> 00:40:43.550
and yet it's so fundamentally
unfair and wrong

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:46.623
the way resources are shared or hoarded.

00:40:47.604 --> 00:40:50.980
- Yeah, absolutely and as you
say, your research in Greece

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:53.300
demonstrates this remarkably clearly.

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:55.300
So this is a tension I think that arises

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:59.680
in so much of our research,
where we, we recognize,

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:01.810
of course the agency that people have

00:41:01.810 --> 00:41:04.220
and we, in my case, want to document

00:41:04.220 --> 00:41:06.680
the work that people are
doing and they are resisting

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:08.680
and they are archiving their own responses

00:41:08.680 --> 00:41:11.630
and they're doing the research
and they're demonstrating

00:41:11.630 --> 00:41:13.630
their capabilities to conceptualize

00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:15.950
and to develop different
systems, et cetera, et cetera,

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:18.620
not just to survive and to stay alive,

00:41:18.620 --> 00:41:22.620
that's not the basis of, of their actions

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:24.770
and yet at the same time, this recognition

00:41:24.770 --> 00:41:26.310
that, it's not sustainable.

00:41:26.310 --> 00:41:30.230
So if you think about
the, the image that I had

00:41:30.230 --> 00:41:31.640
of people from Nahr el-Bared,

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:34.420
these are internally displaced refugees,

00:41:34.420 --> 00:41:36.870
hosted by other refugees,
now hosting refugees.

00:41:36.870 --> 00:41:40.510
If we recognize displacement
as being part of this

00:41:40.510 --> 00:41:43.470
always already being in
the middle of displacement,

00:41:43.470 --> 00:41:44.440
then it's thinking through,

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:47.750
well, what are the political
foundations for that crisis,

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:51.050
those crises, the overlapping precarities

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:54.170
and how do we in fact need
a deeply political response

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:57.330
to this rather than the
constant de-politicization

00:41:57.330 --> 00:41:58.720
of humanitarian crisis

00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:01.470
as if another plaster is
going to really fix it.

00:42:01.470 --> 00:42:02.750
So I think that it goes back

00:42:02.750 --> 00:42:07.383
to what, I think that
Catherine was identifying

00:42:07.383 --> 00:42:09.280
that I was stressing on the structural

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:11.810
and I think that I am
stressing on the structural,

00:42:11.810 --> 00:42:13.940
precisely because of that
tension in my own research

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:16.770
that I don't want to
suggest that, that the US

00:42:16.770 --> 00:42:19.290
or that the UN has the right to withdraw

00:42:19.290 --> 00:42:21.970
and to say, "We don't have
to be involved anymore,

00:42:21.970 --> 00:42:23.310
we don't have a responsibility

00:42:23.310 --> 00:42:25.800
'cause look, people are
doing it themselves",

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:28.020
which is effectively one of the
tensions of the localization

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:30.280
of aid agenda, saying,
"Yes, local communities

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:32.530
are doing this,
municipalities can do this"

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:35.050
but they need the
relevant forms of support,

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:37.790
not an abrogation of responsibility.

00:42:37.790 --> 00:42:42.400
So for me, it's that
combination of documenting

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:45.770
what, archiving what people
are always already doing

00:42:45.770 --> 00:42:48.280
but highlighting that
this is not sustainable,

00:42:48.280 --> 00:42:50.920
precisely because it's,
if it's not gonna be COVID

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:52.530
and it's not gonna be the Beirut blast,

00:42:52.530 --> 00:42:54.210
it's going to be something else

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:57.830
and you can't expect people
to continue carrying that

00:42:57.830 --> 00:43:00.610
without addressing those
structural inequalities.

00:43:00.610 --> 00:43:02.560
I'm not giving you any ethnographic detail

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:04.640
in that response Zareena

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:07.620
but I know that you, you
have done so much research

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:08.580
in this area as well

00:43:08.580 --> 00:43:10.410
and I think it's more,
it's more that tension,

00:43:10.410 --> 00:43:12.170
even what's the ethics,
what are the ethics

00:43:12.170 --> 00:43:14.470
of even documenting what people are doing?

00:43:14.470 --> 00:43:17.570
How does that play into and
potentially reproduce the system

00:43:17.570 --> 00:43:19.750
of abrogating responsibility,

00:43:19.750 --> 00:43:22.833
when these are intrinsically
political contexts?

00:43:23.740 --> 00:43:25.040
- It's an unfair question

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:27.650
because honestly it's something
I myself have not resolved

00:43:27.650 --> 00:43:32.650
in my own work of not
wanting to kind of idealize

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:35.343
this incredible resistance,

00:43:36.690 --> 00:43:38.990
without kind of that kind
of hard-hitting critique

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:41.170
and I think you do, I learn
from you always, Elena

00:43:41.170 --> 00:43:44.030
'cause you do it I think very
effectively here and elsewhere

00:43:44.030 --> 00:43:46.400
where you really do
manage to keep us thinking

00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:47.950
about both scales.

00:43:47.950 --> 00:43:51.557
- [Elena] That's really kind
of used Zareena, thank you.

00:43:51.557 --> 00:43:52.660
- Can I just jump in there?

00:43:52.660 --> 00:43:55.100
And I think, I mean, I'm
really loving this conversation

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:56.700
but I think that's maybe
what I was interested in

00:43:56.700 --> 00:43:58.280
in terms of that structural

00:43:58.280 --> 00:44:02.260
'cause I agree completely
with (murmurs) and I recognize

00:44:02.260 --> 00:44:04.680
in the organizations that I work with here

00:44:04.680 --> 00:44:07.970
in the Asia Pacific, the
same, the same themes

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:09.200
and the same frustrations

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:11.170
and the same sort of
you're wanting to highlight

00:44:11.170 --> 00:44:14.350
what they do because in
some ways it's breathtaking,

00:44:14.350 --> 00:44:17.150
like really creative and wonderful

00:44:17.150 --> 00:44:19.160
but other times it's so frustrating

00:44:19.160 --> 00:44:24.160
to think that how much work is
and how much really hard work

00:44:24.380 --> 00:44:28.600
is done to try and even
get mega things happening.

00:44:28.600 --> 00:44:30.970
So my question is really,
how do we change the systems

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:32.940
as well as recognize the strengths

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:37.760
and I think that how the strategically

00:44:37.760 --> 00:44:40.380
and not in, and I think
you're right, Elena

00:44:40.380 --> 00:44:42.917
in terms of like, is it
really representation

00:44:42.917 --> 00:44:45.370
and what does it look
like and who's speaking

00:44:45.370 --> 00:44:47.940
and all those sorts of things
which are very complicated

00:44:47.940 --> 00:44:50.500
and is it actually shifting anything?

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:53.760
But that's my real
question is how do we take

00:44:53.760 --> 00:44:57.620
what is done at a local
level and also change systems

00:44:57.620 --> 00:45:01.530
and recognize that we don't
want to shift responsibility

00:45:01.530 --> 00:45:04.317
but bring people into
that picture, so anyway.

00:45:05.390 --> 00:45:07.593
- Thank you, Louise and
absolutely, I don't think

00:45:07.593 --> 00:45:09.720
that I have the solution, certainly

00:45:09.720 --> 00:45:11.940
but I'm also not sure that
I should have the solution,

00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:14.440
that's exactly the point,
is that it shouldn't be me

00:45:14.440 --> 00:45:18.030
in Oxford, in the UK saying,
"This is what the UN should do"

00:45:18.030 --> 00:45:20.420
but rather actually,
well, how do we listen?

00:45:20.420 --> 00:45:23.520
How do we actually stop
and listen and deconstruct

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:28.010
and actually start an open
and equitable conversation

00:45:28.010 --> 00:45:29.540
around this with the people

00:45:29.540 --> 00:45:31.620
who are not just affected communities,

00:45:31.620 --> 00:45:34.150
they are always already responding

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:37.470
and the humanitarian system
often undermines those responses

00:45:37.470 --> 00:45:38.630
but won't accept that

00:45:38.630 --> 00:45:42.080
because it's also part of
the survival of the system,

00:45:42.080 --> 00:45:45.530
which has at its core,
the foundation or the aim

00:45:45.530 --> 00:45:47.853
of improving people's lives.

00:45:49.010 --> 00:45:50.990
But I think that that,
that's the ongoing work,

00:45:50.990 --> 00:45:53.750
it's how to open up those
equitable conversations

00:45:53.750 --> 00:45:57.710
in a way that is willing
to, to challenge the systems

00:45:57.710 --> 00:46:00.410
and we can go around in circles on it

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:03.750
but I'm, I'm not the
right person to answer it

00:46:04.840 --> 00:46:06.760
and, but neither is necessarily one person

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:08.300
from Baddawi Refugee Camp,

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:10.340
it's how we actually join up those dots

00:46:10.340 --> 00:46:12.090
and how we have perhaps those examples

00:46:12.090 --> 00:46:15.450
of horizontal conversations
and seeing what works

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:17.420
and, what works to destabilize

00:46:17.420 --> 00:46:19.393
as well as what works to, to construct.

00:46:27.410 --> 00:46:29.200
I think Paul had a question...

00:46:30.050 --> 00:46:33.100
- Yes, hi Elena, thank
you for the presentation,

00:46:33.100 --> 00:46:35.510
I joined late, I couldn't
actually connect,

00:46:35.510 --> 00:46:39.760
I'm in Lebanon now and
actually I, we went through,

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:41.983
me and my family through the Beirut blast.

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:45.240
However, I don't wanna go there

00:46:45.240 --> 00:46:47.873
because that's, that's a, that
would be a long discussion.

00:46:49.722 --> 00:46:52.320
I'm currently finalizing
my PhD at the UCL,

00:46:52.320 --> 00:46:57.320
I'm a UCL, Yale scholar
and my ethnographic studies

00:46:57.650 --> 00:47:00.420
and my investigation is
about Syrian refugees.

00:47:00.420 --> 00:47:03.950
So I know that they are very marginalized,

00:47:03.950 --> 00:47:07.010
I know that to some findings,

00:47:07.010 --> 00:47:11.550
that some bottom-up approaches
were successful in a way,

00:47:11.550 --> 00:47:13.000
even if temporary

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:15.330
to,

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:17.210
let's say, strengthen the ties

00:47:17.210 --> 00:47:20.410
between host community
members and senior refugees.

00:47:20.410 --> 00:47:21.580
However, when I visited

00:47:21.580 --> 00:47:23.630
and I actually have a paper about that

00:47:23.630 --> 00:47:27.030
and I did some ethnographic
studies on the Dbayeh Camp,

00:47:27.030 --> 00:47:28.590
where you have Palestinian

00:47:28.590 --> 00:47:31.910
and then Syrian refugees were recently,

00:47:31.910 --> 00:47:35.160
I mean, they recently
joined after the crisis,

00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:37.500
the camp is, is there over 50 years.

00:47:37.500 --> 00:47:39.700
And the same thing for Ein El Hilweh.

00:47:40.940 --> 00:47:44.030
I saw and I noticed through the interviews

00:47:44.030 --> 00:47:45.580
that Syrian refugees over there

00:47:45.580 --> 00:47:48.600
are even marginalized
by Palestinian refugees.

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:53.260
So there is like
refugees-to-refugees marginalization,

00:47:53.260 --> 00:47:55.780
which actually was an interesting finding.

00:47:55.780 --> 00:47:58.650
So the question is
because you're an expert

00:47:58.650 --> 00:48:02.220
in that, more investigating
the Syrian refugees,

00:48:02.220 --> 00:48:04.500
so the question is, have
you found or noticed

00:48:04.500 --> 00:48:07.520
any bottom-up approaches or any mechanisms

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:12.320
between Palestinian and Syrian
refugees within those camps

00:48:12.320 --> 00:48:17.320
to strengthen the, or let's
say lessen the marginalization

00:48:18.080 --> 00:48:19.810
between the two parties?

00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:22.730
For example, this, this club
that you just mentioned,

00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:24.413
does it include Syrian refugees?

00:48:25.451 --> 00:48:26.284
Thank you.

00:48:27.711 --> 00:48:31.180
- Thank you very much, Paul
and I wish that UCL were open

00:48:31.180 --> 00:48:35.060
so that we could meet and
have the conversation there

00:48:35.060 --> 00:48:37.473
but thank you for, thank
you for your question.

00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:41.360
So absolutely, as, as Zareena was saying,

00:48:41.360 --> 00:48:45.140
in my research I don't
mean to idealize responses

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:48.470
from Baddawi Refugee Camp
or any of the other places

00:48:48.470 --> 00:48:53.180
where I speak with people and
listen to what they're saying

00:48:53.180 --> 00:48:56.590
but rather to explore
those complex relationships

00:48:56.590 --> 00:48:59.050
and what it means to actually
focus on the relationship

00:48:59.050 --> 00:49:01.940
between Palestinians and Syrians
or Palestinians and Kurds,

00:49:01.940 --> 00:49:05.500
Palestinians and Iraqis and
the multiple directionalities

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:09.040
of displacement and hosting
that exist in these situations.

00:49:09.040 --> 00:49:10.900
And certainly in my broader research,

00:49:10.900 --> 00:49:13.900
I do document and explore
different instances

00:49:13.900 --> 00:49:17.550
of marginalization, of
discrimination and of exclusion,

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:21.570
so that's certainly part and
parcel of, of those processes.

00:49:21.570 --> 00:49:24.940
There are many different
initiatives that do recognize that

00:49:24.940 --> 00:49:29.130
and explicitly attempt to
promote social cohesion

00:49:29.130 --> 00:49:34.130
in that lovely NGO language,
but also go beyond that

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:37.860
and think through, well, we're
all residents of this space

00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:41.010
and if we take the neighborhood
or the camp as the core,

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:42.930
then it's everybody who's in this space

00:49:42.930 --> 00:49:45.250
who is a member of this organization

00:49:45.250 --> 00:49:47.700
or who is involved in this
particular initiative.

00:49:48.700 --> 00:49:53.190
And there, there are many,
many different local responses

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:54.940
which embody that

00:49:54.940 --> 00:49:59.070
and which attempt to
challenge the discrimination.

00:49:59.070 --> 00:50:02.370
As I was suggesting in
my presentation today,

00:50:02.370 --> 00:50:05.200
discrimination, hostility,
tension, isn't inevitable,

00:50:05.200 --> 00:50:08.170
it's often produced politically.

00:50:08.170 --> 00:50:11.120
So the example that I was
giving from Baddawi Refugee Camp

00:50:11.120 --> 00:50:13.500
was of, I'm not sure at
what stage you joined

00:50:13.500 --> 00:50:16.890
but nationality-based
testing in Tripoli Port

00:50:16.890 --> 00:50:20.070
of Syrian refugees,
identified Syrian refugees

00:50:20.070 --> 00:50:24.747
who lived in Baddawi
Camp as carrying COVID-19

00:50:24.747 --> 00:50:26.210
and of bringing it into the camp

00:50:26.210 --> 00:50:29.760
and of polluting the previously safe camp.

00:50:29.760 --> 00:50:33.050
So here it's nationality-based
testing, media reporting,

00:50:33.050 --> 00:50:35.300
political declarations,

00:50:35.300 --> 00:50:40.040
which leads to the suspicion
of, of Syrians as the epicenter

00:50:40.040 --> 00:50:42.930
and as, as the bearers of the disease.

00:50:42.930 --> 00:50:46.620
So if we recognize that
tensions, hostility, et cetera,

00:50:46.620 --> 00:50:48.430
are produced rather inevitable,

00:50:48.430 --> 00:50:51.010
then that's precisely
how it can be challenged

00:50:51.010 --> 00:50:52.380
and it can be deconstructed.

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:56.580
And again, Zareena's work in
Greece demonstrates the same,

00:50:56.580 --> 00:50:58.200
that you have strange alliances

00:50:58.200 --> 00:51:00.180
or what seem to be strange alliances

00:51:00.180 --> 00:51:03.750
and Zareena can speak to this
herself, across political

00:51:03.750 --> 00:51:07.320
and ideological lines,
from atheist and secular

00:51:07.320 --> 00:51:11.290
to faith-based responses, et cetera.

00:51:11.290 --> 00:51:14.040
So there definitely is marginalization,

00:51:14.040 --> 00:51:15.510
there definitely is discrimination,

00:51:15.510 --> 00:51:18.040
just as there is between
different groups of Palestinians,

00:51:18.040 --> 00:51:20.390
there's discrimination
between Palestinians

00:51:20.390 --> 00:51:22.180
who were born in Baddawi Camp

00:51:22.180 --> 00:51:24.350
and Palestinians who've come from Yarmouk

00:51:24.350 --> 00:51:28.580
and you can identify where, if
it's a Palestinian from Syria

00:51:28.580 --> 00:51:29.910
or a Palestinian from Lebanon

00:51:29.910 --> 00:51:32.550
and there are multiple forms of exclusion

00:51:32.550 --> 00:51:34.000
and marginalization.

00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:36.660
For me, it's recognizing
that it's not inevitable

00:51:36.660 --> 00:51:38.950
and that there are multiple
ways of addressing it

00:51:38.950 --> 00:51:41.170
but it does come back I
think, to the political

00:51:41.170 --> 00:51:43.040
on so many different levels.

00:51:43.040 --> 00:51:44.630
And I think it would be great,

00:51:44.630 --> 00:51:46.640
Zareena would you like to come in on that

00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:48.030
because this is the core

00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:50.630
of so much of your research as well?

00:51:50.630 --> 00:51:52.920
- Yeah, I mean, I, I love this point

00:51:52.920 --> 00:51:54.960
and you know, it seems like
such a simple point to say

00:51:54.960 --> 00:51:59.260
that that these conflicts
are produced and not natural

00:51:59.260 --> 00:52:02.510
and yet, when we look at
the way that the NGOs think

00:52:02.510 --> 00:52:04.330
and the way governments think,

00:52:04.330 --> 00:52:07.530
they absolutely treat these
conflicts between refugees

00:52:07.530 --> 00:52:10.660
as spontaneous and natural
eruptions in the camps,

00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:11.493
rather than things

00:52:11.493 --> 00:52:13.470
that are absolutely
produced by structures.

00:52:13.470 --> 00:52:15.720
And so, I think as scholar sometimes,

00:52:15.720 --> 00:52:17.787
I mean, I certainly have the
feeling where I'm just like,

00:52:17.787 --> 00:52:19.150
"This is such a banal point,

00:52:19.150 --> 00:52:21.030
should I keep making this point?"

00:52:21.030 --> 00:52:23.460
And then I hear Elena make
it so powerfully again

00:52:23.460 --> 00:52:24.800
and I say, "Oh my God, yes"

00:52:24.800 --> 00:52:26.880
because people don't get
this point, they think

00:52:26.880 --> 00:52:30.960
that, I mean, in the camp that
I was working in in Greece,

00:52:30.960 --> 00:52:34.480
it was that the strollers go to the Kurds,

00:52:34.480 --> 00:52:35.490
before they go to the Afghanis,

00:52:35.490 --> 00:52:37.410
before they go to somebody else

00:52:37.410 --> 00:52:39.320
and then, Oh, well, why that?

00:52:39.320 --> 00:52:42.513
And well my, I had a baby
and I needed a stroller now

00:52:42.513 --> 00:52:43.950
who, who decides that?

00:52:43.950 --> 00:52:46.250
Why can't it just simply be,

00:52:46.250 --> 00:52:48.110
whoever needs a stroller gets a stroller?

00:52:48.110 --> 00:52:50.910
What does this invocation
of nationality do?

00:52:50.910 --> 00:52:54.240
And I think, I mean, with
the COVID, it's so powerful

00:52:54.240 --> 00:52:57.330
to think of the ways in
which they're both...

00:52:58.850 --> 00:52:59.683
- [Elena] Thank you, Zareena.

00:52:59.683 --> 00:53:01.800
I don't know if we got
cut off just at the end

00:53:03.480 --> 00:53:04.313
but thank you.

00:53:04.313 --> 00:53:05.483
Paul, what, what do you,

00:53:06.930 --> 00:53:09.663
what do you think of that
response or those responses?

00:53:10.640 --> 00:53:11.473
- I mean,

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:16.860
the, I mean, it was obvious
that the NGOs are exacerbating

00:53:16.860 --> 00:53:19.200
the whole tension within the camp

00:53:19.200 --> 00:53:21.743
and within the host community members.

00:53:22.840 --> 00:53:25.790
But I was just curious to
see what, what mechanism

00:53:25.790 --> 00:53:28.000
could have been done to alleviate that

00:53:28.000 --> 00:53:29.700
between Palestinian and Syrian refugees

00:53:29.700 --> 00:53:32.000
or other refugees within the camps

00:53:32.000 --> 00:53:34.930
'cause I didn't notice that
much of bottom-up approaches

00:53:34.930 --> 00:53:37.893
or top-down approaches in
that that were successful.

00:53:39.290 --> 00:53:42.620
However, actually based
on, on also my research

00:53:42.620 --> 00:53:45.277
regarding COVID-19,

00:53:45.277 --> 00:53:49.230
Syrian refugees were
actually marginalized,

00:53:49.230 --> 00:53:51.170
I'm talking about the Beqaa area,

00:53:51.170 --> 00:53:52.773
this is where my research is.

00:53:54.140 --> 00:53:57.070
They were afraid from the
host community members

00:53:57.070 --> 00:53:59.620
to be stigmatized and all that

00:53:59.620 --> 00:54:03.700
and then actually no cases,
no cases were reported.

00:54:03.700 --> 00:54:07.040
So it was the, it was the opposite way

00:54:07.040 --> 00:54:11.260
and they, they worked very
well with the municipality,

00:54:11.260 --> 00:54:13.910
they were able to coordinate very well,

00:54:13.910 --> 00:54:17.900
I mean, the shawish or, I
mean, they were able to find,

00:54:17.900 --> 00:54:21.450
to find those internal
structural organizations

00:54:21.450 --> 00:54:22.700
that communicate with authorities.

00:54:22.700 --> 00:54:26.560
So it was very successful,
I mean, till now

00:54:26.560 --> 00:54:30.450
and now we have more cases,
that's, it's a nationwide.

00:54:30.450 --> 00:54:34.773
But I didn't see yet any successful,

00:54:36.380 --> 00:54:40.450
let's say, mechanisms that that
brought the social cohesion

00:54:40.450 --> 00:54:41.550
or social integration.

00:54:42.770 --> 00:54:44.870
- Yeah, I think it also
comes back to that question

00:54:44.870 --> 00:54:47.270
of when you have to step
in to alleviate something

00:54:47.270 --> 00:54:49.310
and then what Zareena and I are suggesting

00:54:49.310 --> 00:54:50.940
is that it's actually useful to go back

00:54:50.940 --> 00:54:53.140
and to think about how
and why was this produced

00:54:53.140 --> 00:54:56.340
to begin with, rather than
assuming that there is a problem

00:54:56.340 --> 00:55:00.430
that needs to be fixed by
an external intervention

00:55:00.430 --> 00:55:03.040
and, so I think that's,
that's one response

00:55:03.040 --> 00:55:04.760
or one thought process that I have

00:55:04.760 --> 00:55:07.260
in relation to, to what
you're just saying there.

00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:09.460
And then I'm just gonna bring up a slide

00:55:09.460 --> 00:55:11.190
from another presentation that I have here

00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:13.230
in terms of that notion of success

00:55:13.230 --> 00:55:15.390
and what mechanisms might be successful.

00:55:15.390 --> 00:55:18.760
So Zareena has seen
this from a presentation

00:55:18.760 --> 00:55:23.030
that I gave at Yale a
year, two years ago now.

00:55:23.030 --> 00:55:27.520
So this notion of what is
visible and what is undisclosed.

00:55:27.520 --> 00:55:29.300
So a lot of our initiatives,

00:55:29.300 --> 00:55:31.440
sorry, a lot of the initiatives
that we've been documenting

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:34.430
are kind of below the
radar to a certain extent,

00:55:34.430 --> 00:55:37.590
so here what I call the
"poetics of undisclosed care"

00:55:37.590 --> 00:55:40.287
are documented through these
three interview extracts,

00:55:40.287 --> 00:55:42.130
"So we collected clothes, we offered food

00:55:42.130 --> 00:55:44.570
and cash to refugees but I
hope you don't mention this

00:55:44.570 --> 00:55:46.470
except for reasons
related to your research

00:55:46.470 --> 00:55:48.640
because we do this only for God's sake".

00:55:48.640 --> 00:55:52.010
So that's a resident of Baddawi
Camp from Nahr el-Bared.

00:55:52.010 --> 00:55:53.757
A Syrian refugee living in Baddawi Camp,

00:55:53.757 --> 00:55:55.090
"Those people who offer assistance

00:55:55.090 --> 00:55:57.950
without disclosing their
names deserve respect"

00:55:57.950 --> 00:56:00.657
and a Kurdish refugee from
Syria, living in Baddawi Camp,

00:56:00.657 --> 00:56:02.350
"Be like the good fruit that gives...",

00:56:02.350 --> 00:56:05.090
sorry, "Be like the good
tree that gives its fruits

00:56:05.090 --> 00:56:06.950
and does not ask who took them".

00:56:06.950 --> 00:56:09.750
So how do we think about
what is successful,

00:56:09.750 --> 00:56:13.990
what is visible, what is
invisible in these contexts?

00:56:13.990 --> 00:56:16.030
And there's definitely
differentiated care,

00:56:16.030 --> 00:56:17.757
here's another, another quote here,

00:56:17.757 --> 00:56:19.470
"The differential response

00:56:19.470 --> 00:56:22.530
to residents of Nahr
el-Bared Camp being displaced

00:56:22.530 --> 00:56:26.250
to Baddawi Camp, as opposed
to those displaced from Syria,

00:56:26.250 --> 00:56:28.650
something that's very well documented

00:56:28.650 --> 00:56:30.937
and kind of discussed within the camp",

00:56:32.280 --> 00:56:35.150
again, another example
of differentiated care

00:56:35.150 --> 00:56:39.260
and then what I'm looking for
here is what we can think of

00:56:39.260 --> 00:56:41.030
as that notion of success,

00:56:41.030 --> 00:56:42.700
which is coming back to
that question that you have.

00:56:42.700 --> 00:56:45.940
So here is a Syrian who
is living in Baddawi Camp

00:56:45.940 --> 00:56:48.350
and they say, "The local
communities here in Baddawi Camp

00:56:48.350 --> 00:56:50.577
are also in a deplorable situation

00:56:50.577 --> 00:56:52.430
and also suffer from poverty.

00:56:52.430 --> 00:56:53.780
They cannot meet their own needs,

00:56:53.780 --> 00:56:55.370
how can they provide in-kind

00:56:55.370 --> 00:56:57.280
and financial assistance to others?

00:56:57.280 --> 00:56:59.400
We only ask them for good treatment

00:56:59.400 --> 00:57:01.440
and non-discrimination between refugees

00:57:01.440 --> 00:57:03.440
on the basis of their
political affiliations",

00:57:03.440 --> 00:57:04.930
which was something
that had been happening

00:57:04.930 --> 00:57:06.817
in Baddawi Camp at that time.

00:57:06.817 --> 00:57:09.170
"The institutions here are
meant to provide assistance

00:57:09.170 --> 00:57:10.710
and we would like them
to provide assistance

00:57:10.710 --> 00:57:12.080
in a profession manner."

00:57:12.080 --> 00:57:17.070
So this idea of what, what
is considered to be enough,

00:57:17.070 --> 00:57:21.500
another interview with
another Syrian in Baddawi Camp

00:57:21.500 --> 00:57:23.770
about, which is kind of
picking up on this notion

00:57:23.770 --> 00:57:25.357
of caring or observing,

00:57:25.357 --> 00:57:27.420
"I think that the biggest
part of the local community

00:57:27.420 --> 00:57:30.290
does not care about this and
their role does not transgress

00:57:30.290 --> 00:57:32.540
the limits of observing".

00:57:32.540 --> 00:57:36.370
So the question that arises
for me is, who determines

00:57:36.370 --> 00:57:39.650
what is enough and what is a good response

00:57:39.650 --> 00:57:41.940
or not, a not good enough response?

00:57:41.940 --> 00:57:45.010
So if we bear in mind the
poetics of undisclosed care

00:57:45.010 --> 00:57:48.000
on the one hand, what
is the necessity of care

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:50.310
versus observing, for example?

00:57:50.310 --> 00:57:52.580
Is it enough to observe?

00:57:52.580 --> 00:57:55.390
This Kurdish refugee from
Syria says, "It is enough

00:57:55.390 --> 00:57:56.940
that they allowed us to live amongst them,

00:57:56.940 --> 00:57:59.310
despite this great population pressure.

00:57:59.310 --> 00:58:01.330
In my view, the local
community is not interested

00:58:01.330 --> 00:58:03.190
in providing us with assistance,

00:58:03.190 --> 00:58:05.870
all they have to do is accept
our presence in these areas

00:58:05.870 --> 00:58:07.710
and to offer us moral support.

00:58:07.710 --> 00:58:08.990
For me, it is enough

00:58:08.990 --> 00:58:11.340
that my Palestinian neighbor
greets me every morning

00:58:11.340 --> 00:58:13.990
and that I go to work, being
sure that my children are well

00:58:13.990 --> 00:58:16.220
amongst their foreign neighbors".

00:58:16.220 --> 00:58:18.650
So it's not a response
but it's a question,

00:58:18.650 --> 00:58:21.810
who decides what is enough, who decides

00:58:21.810 --> 00:58:25.240
if it's material support,
if it's spiritual support,

00:58:25.240 --> 00:58:27.650
if it's greeting somebody in the alleyway,

00:58:27.650 --> 00:58:30.680
sharing that space, ensuring
that the children are well

00:58:30.680 --> 00:58:32.300
amongst their foreign neighbors?

00:58:32.300 --> 00:58:35.480
Is being with or being together enough,

00:58:35.480 --> 00:58:39.070
from whose perspective and
how do we counteract that

00:58:39.070 --> 00:58:41.390
when we think about how NGOs come in

00:58:41.390 --> 00:58:43.357
and say that what we
need is social cohesion

00:58:43.357 --> 00:58:45.770
and what we need is this kind of response,

00:58:45.770 --> 00:58:47.740
this is what success looks like?

00:58:47.740 --> 00:58:49.430
Well, actually, what does it look like

00:58:49.430 --> 00:58:52.130
from conditions of structural precarity?

00:58:52.130 --> 00:58:55.080
What does it look like
from, from the perspective

00:58:55.080 --> 00:58:56.900
of different people within the camps?

00:58:56.900 --> 00:58:59.720
And that's not to say that the,
I agree that that's enough,

00:58:59.720 --> 00:59:02.920
I might have an idealized
vision of what would work better

00:59:03.970 --> 00:59:07.480
but it's a more complex process,
I think, than identifying,

00:59:07.480 --> 00:59:10.180
this is the right
mechanism, this is success,

00:59:10.180 --> 00:59:11.980
success according to whom,

00:59:11.980 --> 00:59:15.090
success according to what
markers of visibility and action?

00:59:15.090 --> 00:59:17.550
I think, that's some of
the questions that I have

00:59:17.550 --> 00:59:20.470
as I'm continuing to go, to read through

00:59:20.470 --> 00:59:22.270
the interview transcripts that we have

00:59:22.270 --> 00:59:24.770
and bearing in mind the
ethnographic research

00:59:24.770 --> 00:59:27.670
that myself and my teams
have been doing as well.

00:59:27.670 --> 00:59:29.610
So it will be really
fascinating to hear more

00:59:29.610 --> 00:59:32.653
about your research as it,
as it continues to develop.

00:59:33.490 --> 00:59:36.660
So thanks for that question
and that conversation

00:59:36.660 --> 00:59:39.300
and it's really lovely to be
able to have that discussion

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:40.330
and that exchange.

00:59:40.330 --> 00:59:41.163
Thanks, Paul.

00:59:43.500 --> 00:59:45.310
- I have a question, Elena,

00:59:45.310 --> 00:59:49.210
it's like, what have
been like the implication

00:59:50.530 --> 00:59:52.200
or what you have seen

00:59:52.200 --> 00:59:57.200
in terms of gender differences basically,

00:59:57.750 --> 01:00:02.190
some of the marginalization of women

01:00:02.190 --> 01:00:07.190
that you have seen before,
how that has changed now

01:00:10.013 --> 01:00:13.853
or, I mean, what directions
with the pandemic?

01:00:15.480 --> 01:00:20.480
And the other thing is about now

01:00:22.120 --> 01:00:27.120
in terms of the vaccinations
because I guess that even,

01:00:28.520 --> 01:00:31.580
I guess in every country we're seeing now

01:00:31.580 --> 01:00:36.580
sort of some groups being put
like at the end of the queue.

01:00:37.140 --> 01:00:41.110
So how you have seen that basically

01:00:42.420 --> 01:00:47.220
from the, a country that
is most likely stretched

01:00:47.220 --> 01:00:51.480
in terms of vaccinating its own citizens,

01:00:51.480 --> 01:00:52.800
how,

01:00:52.800 --> 01:00:57.800
well, the xenophobic views
are probably reinforced there

01:00:59.520 --> 01:01:02.617
in term, with, with the
vaccination now (murmurs)?

01:01:04.240 --> 01:01:05.750
- Great, thank you, Teresa.

01:01:05.750 --> 01:01:09.330
So I haven't looked
specifically at gender dynamics

01:01:09.330 --> 01:01:13.890
with reference to COVID,
I've, I haven't been able

01:01:13.890 --> 01:01:17.370
to be in Baddawi Camp
obviously for a long time,

01:01:17.370 --> 01:01:20.280
so it's kind of, it's all from a distance,

01:01:20.280 --> 01:01:24.330
suddenly there are
longstanding unequal roles

01:01:25.420 --> 01:01:28.540
played by women with
reference to the destruction

01:01:28.540 --> 01:01:31.580
of particular key services in
a, in a space like Baddawi,

01:01:31.580 --> 01:01:33.920
so if we think about school closures,

01:01:33.920 --> 01:01:37.260
so women are actively involved
in educating their children

01:01:37.260 --> 01:01:40.810
and supporting their learning
with often multiple children

01:01:40.810 --> 01:01:43.910
of different ages, attempting
to access online learning

01:01:43.910 --> 01:01:47.630
through the one mobile phone

01:01:47.630 --> 01:01:49.410
or the radio system

01:01:49.410 --> 01:01:51.180
that might possibly be giving them access,

01:01:51.180 --> 01:01:53.510
so their domestic burdens are increasing,

01:01:53.510 --> 01:01:55.930
their educational provision,

01:01:55.930 --> 01:01:58.830
attempting to keep people safe and clean,

01:01:58.830 --> 01:02:03.830
so all of those duties
are enhanced precisely

01:02:04.330 --> 01:02:09.150
because of the pandemic,
the ability to, to make due

01:02:09.150 --> 01:02:13.580
with very small amounts of food available,

01:02:13.580 --> 01:02:18.200
how you change your entire
domestic framework around cooking

01:02:18.200 --> 01:02:21.540
and keeping your safe and
healthy and fed, et cetera,

01:02:21.540 --> 01:02:24.910
they're all key issues,
large numbers of women

01:02:24.910 --> 01:02:28.010
who are formal educators as well

01:02:28.010 --> 01:02:32.180
and therefore are teaching
just as many of us are teaching

01:02:32.180 --> 01:02:34.833
plus looking after our
families at home, et cetera.

01:02:36.070 --> 01:02:37.070
And the impact

01:02:37.070 --> 01:02:40.050
of the reduction of
informal labor opportunities

01:02:40.050 --> 01:02:43.120
obviously leaving men in
particularly vulnerable positions

01:02:43.120 --> 01:02:44.440
to exploitation as well,

01:02:44.440 --> 01:02:47.600
which has certainly been
documented in, in many instances.

01:02:47.600 --> 01:02:49.230
So Syrian refugees, for example,

01:02:49.230 --> 01:02:52.210
who were already male refugees,
who were already at risk

01:02:52.210 --> 01:02:55.780
of detention, deportation,
expulsion, et cetera,

01:02:55.780 --> 01:02:59.620
are even more at risk now
precisely because of curfews,

01:02:59.620 --> 01:03:01.480
because of lockdowns,

01:03:01.480 --> 01:03:03.980
because of those people who
are or don't have the privilege

01:03:03.980 --> 01:03:05.730
of remaining in doors

01:03:05.730 --> 01:03:10.470
and being, respecting
social distancing rules,

01:03:10.470 --> 01:03:12.200
being more visible in the public sphere

01:03:12.200 --> 01:03:13.650
and therefore more at risk.

01:03:13.650 --> 01:03:15.620
So I think there are
different, different ways

01:03:15.620 --> 01:03:18.110
in which gender relations
have been affected

01:03:18.110 --> 01:03:20.210
and particular gender vulnerabilities

01:03:20.210 --> 01:03:24.210
have been accentuated by, by COVID-19.

01:03:24.210 --> 01:03:26.380
With reference to vaccinations in Lebanon

01:03:26.380 --> 01:03:28.730
and I'm sure that Paul
will be able to give us

01:03:28.730 --> 01:03:33.580
kind of more up-to-date briefing
from Beirut perspective,

01:03:33.580 --> 01:03:36.250
I think a lot of us were very
concerned that Palestinians

01:03:36.250 --> 01:03:39.450
and Syrians would be excluded
from the vaccination rollout

01:03:39.450 --> 01:03:42.060
but in fact, shortly after
the Lebanese politicians

01:03:42.060 --> 01:03:44.970
were first vaccinated in the country,

01:03:44.970 --> 01:03:49.700
vaccines were reaching UNHCR
and UNRWA distribution points,

01:03:49.700 --> 01:03:53.350
so Palestinian refugees
have and Syrian refugees

01:03:53.350 --> 01:03:55.780
have been able to
register for vaccinations,

01:03:55.780 --> 01:03:58.160
there has been quite a bit of reticence

01:03:58.160 --> 01:04:02.020
amongst Lebanese citizens, so
although there has been a push

01:04:02.020 --> 01:04:05.200
to vaccinate Lebanese citizens,

01:04:05.200 --> 01:04:07.500
the uptake potentially hasn't been as high

01:04:07.500 --> 01:04:10.230
as many would have
expected and there's been

01:04:11.472 --> 01:04:13.160
a particular buyout system

01:04:13.160 --> 01:04:17.070
to ensure that UN systems
were able to purchase

01:04:17.070 --> 01:04:19.220
and distribute those vaccines.

01:04:19.220 --> 01:04:22.480
So thankfully, there
has been a recognition

01:04:22.480 --> 01:04:26.830
that the public health crisis
requires vaccination of all

01:04:26.830 --> 01:04:30.810
and this has not immediately
materialized as discrimination

01:04:30.810 --> 01:04:35.060
against Palestinian, Syrian,
Iraqi, Kurdish refugees

01:04:35.060 --> 01:04:37.780
accessing the vaccine,
so there is a rollout,

01:04:37.780 --> 01:04:40.280
we'll have to see how
that, how that continues

01:04:41.210 --> 01:04:44.580
but in, in this case, a relatively small,

01:04:44.580 --> 01:04:46.960
so of course the per
capita ratio of refugees

01:04:46.960 --> 01:04:50.590
per citizen capita, is
very high in Lebanon

01:04:50.590 --> 01:04:53.960
but the overall population
is relatively small

01:04:53.960 --> 01:04:56.280
when compared with many other countries,

01:04:56.280 --> 01:04:58.230
so the capacity to actually rollout

01:04:58.230 --> 01:05:02.480
is hopefully going to be,
well, we can only hope

01:05:02.480 --> 01:05:04.960
that it's going to, going to work

01:05:04.960 --> 01:05:07.053
in a broadly equitable framework.

01:05:08.380 --> 01:05:11.100
So maybe we'll have a better idea of that

01:05:11.100 --> 01:05:15.000
in a couple of weeks, where,
what are we, three, four weeks

01:05:15.000 --> 01:05:17.330
post the arrival of
the vaccine in Lebanon,

01:05:17.330 --> 01:05:19.203
so it is, it is quite early days.

01:05:20.370 --> 01:05:21.203
I'm not sure if Paul,

01:05:21.203 --> 01:05:26.203
if you have a, a more
up-to-date account of that.

01:05:26.773 --> 01:05:28.270
- I mean, the only update

01:05:28.270 --> 01:05:31.830
is like the whole rollout of the vaccine

01:05:31.830 --> 01:05:34.370
is, has been a flop by
the Lebanese government.

01:05:34.370 --> 01:05:36.920
However, it is, everybody is equated,

01:05:36.920 --> 01:05:40.630
so it is open for everybody,
which is actually a good sign

01:05:40.630 --> 01:05:43.360
but we as Lebanese citizens
now we feel ourselves

01:05:43.360 --> 01:05:46.980
as refugees as well within the system.

01:05:46.980 --> 01:05:49.660
So, I mean, when you see that
the deputies of your country

01:05:49.660 --> 01:05:54.250
just got vaccinate, the, I
went to the official platform

01:05:54.250 --> 01:05:55.800
and everybody is waiting.

01:05:55.800 --> 01:06:00.560
So the amount of vaccines that
arrived here is not enough,

01:06:00.560 --> 01:06:05.560
it's very low, but yes, the
good thing is that everybody,

01:06:06.470 --> 01:06:08.900
not only, not only through
vaccinations actually,

01:06:08.900 --> 01:06:11.960
everybody can be admitted
to hospitals if needed,

01:06:11.960 --> 01:06:13.283
the Hariri State Hospital,

01:06:14.120 --> 01:06:17.130
whether you're Lebanese,
Syrian or Palestinian

01:06:17.130 --> 01:06:21.720
but the vaccination is now
it's, it's, it is a flop

01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:24.420
and it is within the corrupt
system of the government.

01:06:29.370 --> 01:06:32.780
- Do we have any more
questions or comments

01:06:32.780 --> 01:06:34.283
or points of conversation?

01:06:38.020 --> 01:06:43.020
- I think probably I would
like to just mention,

01:06:44.310 --> 01:06:47.020
I mean, with respect to the vaccination

01:06:47.020 --> 01:06:50.040
in a completely different
setting in Latin America

01:06:50.040 --> 01:06:54.850
with the Venezuelan refugees,
that actually the...

01:06:55.790 --> 01:06:56.623
because

01:06:59.140 --> 01:07:04.140
non-registered Venezuelans
do not have access

01:07:04.140 --> 01:07:09.140
to the, for example the
Columbian healthcare system,

01:07:09.330 --> 01:07:14.330
the need to vaccinate them just brought

01:07:16.690 --> 01:07:20.220
basically a legalization program now

01:07:20.220 --> 01:07:22.413
of around 1 million people,

01:07:24.974 --> 01:07:26.260
of 1 million refugees.

01:07:26.260 --> 01:07:31.260
So just, I mean, at least
in terms of the offer

01:07:31.670 --> 01:07:35.350
and even if the supply is
flow for, for everybody

01:07:35.350 --> 01:07:40.350
because the vaccination
program is, just started

01:07:40.480 --> 01:07:43.760
as well as in Lebanon, so at least...

01:07:43.760 --> 01:07:47.150
and that was completely political,

01:07:47.150 --> 01:07:49.220
well, not completely political decision

01:07:49.220 --> 01:07:50.690
because the first reaction

01:07:50.690 --> 01:07:53.520
was not to vaccinate the refugees

01:07:53.520 --> 01:07:55.830
but there was like an outcry

01:07:55.830 --> 01:07:58.030
and then that brought to the change.

01:07:58.030 --> 01:07:58.863
So

01:08:00.100 --> 01:08:05.020
I guess probably that
perspectives sort of reassurance

01:08:05.020 --> 01:08:06.433
in that,

01:08:09.840 --> 01:08:14.420
that behavior but maybe as you say,

01:08:14.420 --> 01:08:18.190
maybe working, I mean, see
what is the uptake as well

01:08:18.190 --> 01:08:19.023
because

01:08:20.990 --> 01:08:25.990
even, I mean, in all the
countries we have seen also

01:08:26.400 --> 01:08:28.440
from minorities in general

01:08:28.440 --> 01:08:32.203
so, of a bit of low
take up of the vaccines.

01:08:33.320 --> 01:08:34.467
- Yeah, absolutely

01:08:34.467 --> 01:08:36.770
and the extent to which,
for example, in the UK

01:08:36.770 --> 01:08:38.280
there are grave concerns

01:08:38.280 --> 01:08:43.280
that with the hostile environment
pervading medical settings

01:08:43.660 --> 01:08:47.890
that people who have irregular
status will, will not risk,

01:08:47.890 --> 01:08:52.890
they won't risk having prenatal
or birthing assistance,

01:08:53.180 --> 01:08:57.160
they won't attend for
vaccinations for their infants,

01:08:57.160 --> 01:08:59.120
let alone will they expose themselves

01:08:59.120 --> 01:09:01.540
potentially to detention and deportation

01:09:01.540 --> 01:09:06.190
on the basis of, of accessing
COVID vaccine, vaccines.

01:09:06.190 --> 01:09:08.540
So absolutely the hostile environment,

01:09:08.540 --> 01:09:10.800
the criminalization of people

01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:13.760
with different legal backgrounds creates,

01:09:13.760 --> 01:09:15.490
again, creates these inequalities

01:09:15.490 --> 01:09:19.473
and creates these, these
public health issues,

01:09:20.480 --> 01:09:23.250
so we can, we can only hope
there are increasing countries

01:09:23.250 --> 01:09:27.880
which are ensuring that there
is access, vaccination access

01:09:27.880 --> 01:09:30.243
for people with, with refugee backgrounds.

01:09:31.904 --> 01:09:36.904
But I was seeing and Yvonne
might, might speak to this,

01:09:36.970 --> 01:09:38.320
in Canada for example,

01:09:38.320 --> 01:09:41.310
the adverts that are being circulated,

01:09:41.310 --> 01:09:44.640
Catherine Clark Kazakh has been
discussing this on Twitter,

01:09:44.640 --> 01:09:47.690
refers to, "all Canadians are
eligible for the vaccine",

01:09:47.690 --> 01:09:51.470
which excludes people who
aren't necessarily citizens.

01:09:51.470 --> 01:09:53.280
So even how the terminology is used,

01:09:53.280 --> 01:09:56.080
who is included or excluded,
when in fact migrants

01:09:56.080 --> 01:09:58.020
and refugees are eligible for the vaccine

01:09:58.020 --> 01:09:59.710
but the terminology that is being used

01:09:59.710 --> 01:10:01.943
is exclusion, is excluding people.

01:10:02.830 --> 01:10:06.210
And as I said, I don't know
if Yvonne has any comments

01:10:06.210 --> 01:10:09.350
from her own research as
well or from her reflections

01:10:09.350 --> 01:10:11.760
on the situation in Canada.

01:10:11.760 --> 01:10:15.650
- [Yvonne] Yes, thanks Elena,
for speaking about that.

01:10:15.650 --> 01:10:17.880
Yes, there's some discussion of that

01:10:17.880 --> 01:10:19.760
and then I think in general,

01:10:19.760 --> 01:10:24.180
there's a lot of discourse by
the prime minister of Canada,

01:10:24.180 --> 01:10:26.520
Justin Trudeau, about this narrative

01:10:26.520 --> 01:10:28.430
of "We're all in this together",

01:10:28.430 --> 01:10:31.540
which has upset a lot of people

01:10:31.540 --> 01:10:33.190
because of course we understand

01:10:33.190 --> 01:10:35.330
that we're not all in this together,

01:10:35.330 --> 01:10:38.680
there's a lot of inequalities that exist.

01:10:38.680 --> 01:10:41.920
So this type of discourse
has been upsetting people

01:10:41.920 --> 01:10:45.180
'cause it's been taking place
also at all different levels

01:10:45.180 --> 01:10:47.370
of the government as well.

01:10:47.370 --> 01:10:50.520
And in general, a lot
of vaccine confusion.

01:10:50.520 --> 01:10:54.460
Everybody is, all over the
news everybody is quite upset

01:10:54.460 --> 01:10:55.980
about how it's getting rolled out,

01:10:55.980 --> 01:10:58.720
what systems are being used,
people are trying to line up

01:10:58.720 --> 01:11:01.330
and that's, that's of no help

01:11:01.330 --> 01:11:03.380
'cause they're supposed
to make phone calls,

01:11:03.380 --> 01:11:05.730
you make phone calls and
you can't get through

01:11:05.730 --> 01:11:08.800
'cause everybody is also on
the line trying to sort it out,

01:11:08.800 --> 01:11:11.963
so it, yeah, Canada's not a great example.

01:11:13.850 --> 01:11:15.770
- [Elena] Yep, thank you, Yvonne.

01:11:16.630 --> 01:11:18.590
Again kind of disrupting the assumptions

01:11:18.590 --> 01:11:21.460
that erupted so early
on in the COVID pandemic

01:11:21.460 --> 01:11:23.930
that certain countries
would be well-prepared

01:11:23.930 --> 01:11:25.740
and the red, the red zones

01:11:25.740 --> 01:11:27.930
of where there would be greater crisis

01:11:27.930 --> 01:11:30.610
hasn't necessarily corresponded
with that geopolitics

01:11:30.610 --> 01:11:33.803
of the imagination of,
of health and infirmity.

01:11:38.940 --> 01:11:39.773
- Okay,

01:11:40.749 --> 01:11:44.560
so I think we've reached the end

01:11:44.560 --> 01:11:48.270
of this conversation,
so thank you very much

01:11:48.270 --> 01:11:50.230
for the presentation, Elena

01:11:50.230 --> 01:11:51.063
and

01:11:52.030 --> 01:11:53.850
yes, if, I guess

01:11:53.850 --> 01:11:54.683
we'd like,

01:11:57.320 --> 01:11:58.153
to,

01:11:59.913 --> 01:12:01.292
yeah,

01:12:01.292 --> 01:12:02.125
just to

01:12:05.767 --> 01:12:08.600
contact you on this topic or yeah,

01:12:09.700 --> 01:12:13.760
and also basically sort of expand

01:12:13.760 --> 01:12:16.423
the community of people researching.

01:12:18.126 --> 01:12:19.720
So, thank you very much.

01:12:19.720 --> 01:12:21.680
